SECTION 5: NEW FEATURE - PERSONAL DOCUMENT VAULT
==================================================

Implement a Personal Document Vault that integrates with the existing Veridex profile and document-management workflows.

This is a new feature, but it must not require redesigning the existing website.

The purpose is to let each user save documents in their personal collection, organize them, and reuse them when responding to document requests.

The vault should be part of the existing application, not a separate standalone application.

## 5.1 User experience

A user should be able to:

1. Open their existing profile or the appropriate document-management area.
2. Access their Personal Document Vault.
3. View documents they have saved.
4. Upload a new document or scan a document.
5. Extract text using OCR when supported.
6. Review and correct the extracted information.
7. Save the document.
8. Search and filter their saved documents.
9. Open a document to view its details.
10. Preview or download the original file where supported.
11. Select a saved document when responding to an existing document request.

Use existing cards, tables, forms, buttons, modals, and styling wherever appropriate.

Do not create an entirely new dashboard just for this feature.

Do not duplicate the existing document repository unnecessarily. Reuse existing document-management components and utilities where possible.

## 5.2 Document categories and metadata

Reuse existing document categories if they already exist.

Allow the user to organize documents using the existing category structure.

Where appropriate, retain the following metadata:
- Unique document ID.
- Owner's existing user ID.
- Original filename.
- MIME type.
- File size.
- Document category.
- Date added.
- Extracted OCR text.
- Editable document details.
- OCR processing status.
- Existing verification status, if applicable.
- Existing approval status, if applicable.

Do not invent verification or approval results.

Do not assume that OCR-extracted names, dates, or document types are correct.

Allow the user to correct extracted information before saving.

Keep the original document separate from the editable metadata and extracted text.

## 5.3 Save documents using browser storage

Use IndexedDB for storing document blobs and associated structured records when appropriate.

Use the existing localStorage implementation for small pieces of application state where suitable.

Do not store large document contents directly in localStorage.

Requirements:
- Create a consistent client-side storage utility for vault records.
- Store the original File or Blob in IndexedDB.
- Associate the stored file with its metadata through a stable document ID.
- Keep the document owner's existing user ID.
- Avoid duplicate records when a document is edited or reused.
- Handle unavailable or full browser storage gracefully.
- Handle missing file blobs without crashing the application.
- Avoid storing large Base64 strings in localStorage.
- Release temporary object URLs when they are no longer required.
- Avoid unnecessary file conversions or duplicate copies.

Use backward-compatible changes to the existing document workflow.

If a selected file has not been saved successfully, do not display it as a permanently saved vault document.

## 5.4 Persistence limitations

This is a frontend-only prototype.

Documents stored in IndexedDB should normally remain available after a page refresh or browser restart, provided the browser retains the site's data.

However:
- Browser storage can be cleared.
- Browser storage is not a guaranteed backup.
- Saved documents will not automatically synchronize between separate browsers or devices.
- Separate users do not automatically share IndexedDB records.
- A browser-local document vault is not a production document-storage service.

Do not claim that files are permanently stored or synchronized across users.

Explain these limitations in the final report.

