SECTION 6: NEW FEATURE - OCR DOCUMENT SCANNING
==================================================

Implement client-side OCR so users can extract readable text from supported documents without creating a backend.

Prefer a browser-based OCR library such as Tesseract.js if it can be integrated safely into the existing project.

Official documentation:
https://tesseract.projectnaptha.com/

Do not call external OCR APIs or upload document contents to external services.

Do not add API keys or credentials.

Do not create a backend OCR endpoint.

## 6.1 Supported document inputs

Support the existing application's compatible upload formats and, where practical:
- JPG and JPEG images.
- PNG images.
- PDFs containing selectable text.
- Scanned PDFs, if reliable browser-side page rendering and OCR can be implemented without destabilizing the project.

Do not promise support for a format unless it is actually implemented.

If scanned PDF processing requires an additional library, evaluate the dependency and its browser compatibility before adding it.

Avoid unnecessary packages.

## 6.2 Upload workflow

The user should be able to select a supported document from their device.

The application should:
1. Validate the selected file type.
2. Validate the file size against a sensible configurable limit.
3. Display the filename and relevant file details.
4. Allow the user to preview the document where supported.
5. Provide an OCR action or initiate OCR according to the existing interface.
6. Display a clear processing state.
7. Display extracted text when processing finishes.
8. Allow the user to correct extracted information.
9. Allow the user to save the original file and associated metadata in their vault.

Handle unsupported files and unreadable documents gracefully.

Do not crash when OCR fails.

## 6.3 OCR processing

For images, use browser-based OCR.

For text-based PDFs, extract embedded text when possible instead of running OCR unnecessarily.

For scanned PDFs, implement page rendering and OCR only if this can be done reliably in the browser with an appropriate dependency.

Requirements:
- Display processing progress where practical.
- Prevent accidental duplicate OCR jobs.
- Allow the user to cancel processing where practical.
- Avoid blocking the entire interface during long-running OCR operations.
- Handle large files and unreadable images gracefully.
- Release temporary resources when processing finishes.
- Do not save extracted data as verified facts.
- Do not mark a document as approved when OCR succeeds.

OCR may misread names, numbers, dates, and other information.

Display extracted information for user review and correction.

Do not silently assume that the extracted information is correct.

## 6.4 Camera-based scanning

If browser camera access can be implemented safely without changing the existing design, support a scan option using the browser's camera interface.

Requirements:
- Request camera permission only when the user explicitly chooses to scan.
- Handle denied permissions and unavailable cameras gracefully.
- Allow the user to capture an image.
- Provide a preview before saving.
- Support basic image adjustments, such as rotation or cropping, if they can be implemented without adding excessive complexity.
- Allow the user to retake a scan.
- Process the resulting image through the existing client-side OCR workflow.

Camera access must be optional.

If reliable camera scanning cannot be implemented safely in the current task, implement file upload and OCR first, and clearly report that camera scanning remains incomplete.

Do not falsely describe ordinary file uploads as camera-based scanning.

## 6.5 Review and correction

After OCR completes, show the extracted text or structured fields in the existing design language.

Allow users to correct relevant information before saving.

Do not automatically infer sensitive document information beyond what is reasonably required by the existing workflow.

Do not treat the OCR result as proof of identity, authenticity, or institutional approval.

Keep OCR status separate from document verification and approval status.

## 6.6 OCR failure handling

Handle:
- Unsupported file types.
- Empty OCR results.
- Unreadable images.
- Corrupted files.
- Failed PDF parsing.
- Browser storage errors.
- OCR processing failures.
- Missing browser capabilities.

Display understandable messages consistent with the current design.

Do not expose internal stack traces to ordinary users.

Do not fabricate extracted text when OCR fails.

Allow the user to retry or save a document without OCR where appropriate.

